Here’s a clear comparison of DELETE, DROP, and TRUNCATE in SQL:

🔸 DELETE
Used to: Remove specific rows from a table.

WHERE clause: ✅ Yes, can use it.

Rollback (Undo): ✅ Yes (if in a transaction).

Triggers: ✅ Fires triggers.

Performance: Slower for large data.

Table structure: Remains intact.

sql
Copy
Edit
DELETE FROM employees WHERE department = 'HR';




🔸 TRUNCATE
Used to: Remove all rows from a table quickly.

WHERE clause: ❌ Not allowed.

Rollback (Undo): ✅ Depends on DB (rollback possible in some RDBMS).

Triggers: ❌ Does not fire triggers (in most databases).

Performance: Faster than DELETE.

Table structure: Remains intact.

sql
Copy
Edit
TRUNCATE TABLE employees;



🔸 DROP
Used to: Completely delete the table (structure + data).

WHERE clause: ❌ Not applicable.

Rollback (Undo): ❌ No, permanent (unless wrapped in transaction-supported RDBMS).

Triggers: ❌ Not applicable.

Performance: Fast.

Table structure: ❌ Removed.

sql
Copy
Edit
DROP TABLE employees;


🧠 Summary Table:
| Feature        | DELETE    | TRUNCATE       | DROP              |
| -------------- | --------- | -------------- | ----------------- |
| Removes data?  | ✅ Yes     | ✅ Yes          | ✅ Yes (and table) |
| WHERE allowed? | ✅ Yes     | ❌ No           | ❌ No              |
| Rollback?      | ✅ Yes     | ⚠️ DB-specific | ❌ No              |
| Triggers fire? | ✅ Yes     | ❌ No           | ❌ No              |
| Keeps schema?  | ✅ Yes     | ✅ Yes          | ❌ No              |
| Speed          | 🐢 Slower | 🚀 Faster      | 🚀 Fastest        |


